      "writtenOn": "2026-07-23T10:35:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The cmount_lock read lock follows the existing pattern used by the\n  cache-related upcall callbacks in this file: ino_invalidate_cb,\n  ino_release_cb, and the directory-invalidation part of this callback.\n\n  It is not intended to serialize a libcephfs mount operation. Export\n  release takes cmount_lock for writing before removing or reassigning\n  cm-\u003ecm_export, or shutting down the final shared cmount. The read lock\n  therefore keeps the export and its upcall vector stable while the upcall\n  is issued. I will also move the up_ops lookup under the read lock to make\n  that lifetime protection explicit.\n\n  As for try_release, the reproduced scenario is a file created through\n  Ganesha and then unlinked by another CephFS client. The MDS moves the\n  inode to the stray directory and libcephfs delivers a delete-style\n  dentry-invalidation callback with the child inode. Ganesha currently\n  invalidates only the parent directory cache, leaving the child MDCACHE\n  entry in place and retaining its FSAL_CEPH handle and libcephfs Inode\n  reference. While that reference remains, the MDS cannot purge the stray\n  inode and its storage remains allocated.\n\n  try_release is used because the object may still legitimately be in use\n  by Ganesha, for example by an open state or an in-flight operation. It\n  removes the cached handle only when the entry is otherwise unused, and\n  returns STILL_IN_USE otherwise. This is also the operation used by\n  ino_release_cb when libcephfs explicitly asks Ganesha to release an inode\n  reference.",
      "parentUuid": "16294448_00e7db1e",
      "revId": "b58275215b5a9e669021e33eef0be09f3e5de75f",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
